WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.100
SpaceX Q2 2026 earning special, revenue reached $7.81 billion, up 92% from a year earlier, in

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.900
the company's first earnings report as a public company.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:20.480
This is SpaceX Daily, episode 59. SpaceX reported revenue of $7.814 billion for the

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:29.150
American quarter, up 92% from $4.071 billion a year ago, and ahead of the roughly $6.9 billion

00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:39.110
analysts had expected. Net loss narrowed to $541 million from the $1.008 billion loss recorded

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:45.250
in the same quarter last year, while loss per share came in at $0.9 against an expected

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:56.090
loss of roughly $0.26. Adjusted E.E. Bittda rose $191% to $3.53 billion, and loss from

00:00:56.090 --> 00:01:04.430
operations improved to $143 million from $970 million, placing the company close to operating

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.410
break even on a quarterly basis.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:14.710
For the first half of the year, the net loss reached $4.817 billion, but that figure includes

00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:23.450
a $1.545 billion loss on debt extinguishment and a $671 million deemed dividend tied to

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:29.730
the pre-IPO capital structure. Those one-time items explain much of the headline six-month

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:35.430
loss and should be set aside when comparing ongoing performance. In the space segment,

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:44.210
revenue reached $962 million, up 29% year over year, and 55% sequentially, while operating

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:53.070
loss widened to $542 million, as starship research and development spending accelerated to $1.076

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:56.830
billion, 55% higher than a year earlier.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:03.950
The company conducted 38 launches in the quarter, 10 for customers and 28 internal, lifting

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:09.970
485 metric tons to orbit. The operating loss grew because development

00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:14.770
costs for the next-generation vehicle outpaced launch revenue growth in the period.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:21.790
Connectivity delivered the clearest profit contribution, with revenue of $4.291 billion,

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:32.250
up 66% year over year, operating income of $1.656 billion, up 79%, and adjusted E. Bittda

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:38.830
of $2.597 billion. Starlink subscribers reached $12.0 million,

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:44.710
doubling from a year earlier and adding $1.7 million in the quarter, while average revenue

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:51.610
per user held steady at $66 per month after the deliberate reduction from $85 a year ago

00:02:51.610 --> 00:03:00.090
to expand the addressable market. Consumer revenue grew 44% to $2.485 billion, while

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:04.790
enterprise and government revenue more than doubled to $1.806 billion.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:16.150
The AI segment posted revenue of $2.561 billion, up 247% year over year, and 213% sequentially

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:21.770
driven by new cloud services agreements that lifted AI solutions and infrastructure revenue

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:30.750
to $2.194 billion, from $311 million. The AI segment recorded its first positive adjusted

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:40.270
E.E. Bittda of $1.146 billion, compared with a negative $276 million a year earlier, even

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:47.270
as it posted an operating loss of $1.257 billion because of heavy depreciation and continued

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:53.630
research and development outlays. Two successful starship V3 flight tests occurred in the quarter,

00:03:54.150 --> 00:04:00.870
flight 12 in May and flight 13 in July, both from the new Starbase pad, flight 12 achieved

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:06.330
the first suborbital mission profile, and a precision upper-stage landing along with

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:12.930
modified V2 Starlink satellite deployment. Flight 13 met all objectives including deployment

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:19.030
of 20 production V3 satellites, an in-space Raptor Relight, and the softest starship

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:25.510
splashdown recorded with an intact heat shield. On the earnings call, Musk stated the next flight,

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:30.610
tentatively scheduled for the end of August, will attempt to catch the ship with the tower

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:36.290
pending regulatory approval and projected a cadence of at least one flight a day, possibly

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:42.090
more within a year. Cloud services agreements totaling $14.1 billion

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:50.010
in contracted sales drove $1.6 billion of incremental AI infrastructure revenue, while nameplate

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:58.010
compute capacity expanded to 1.4 gigawatts from 1.0 gigawatts in the prior quarter and 0.4

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:04.910
gigawatts a year earlier as Colossus 2 construction continued. Reporting on the call noted a Google

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:11.270
agreement for AI capacity and that anthropic contracted all of Colossus 1 capacity in Memphis.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:17.890
The company announced an agreement to acquire cursor for $60 billion expected to close in

00:05:17.890 --> 00:05:22.950
the third quarter of 2026 to accelerate AI enterprise offerings.

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:30.290
GROC 4.5 was released in July as the largest model to date, trained alongside cursor and

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:37.150
incorporating the 1.5 trillion parameter V9 foundation model. More than $6 billion in

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:43.450
multi-year US government star shield contracts were secured. Primarily two space force awards

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:49.650
for low-earth orbit communications and sensing. Additional connectivity wins included an American

00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:56.530
Airlines agreement, plus activations on Southwest, Virgin Atlantic, Iberia, and Airlingus, plus

00:05:56.530 --> 00:06:04.230
mobile partnerships with SoftBank, NTT Dockamo, and SparkNZ, and FCC approval of the EcoStar

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:11.150
license transfer covering 65 megahertz of US spectrum and global mobile satellite service licenses.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:18.330
One thing worth watching is the quarterly capital expenditures that reached $18.369 billion,

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:27.890
of which $15.828 billion went to AI infrastructure, roughly 39% above analyst estimates,

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:32.650
with management indicating the next two quarters could show similar levels.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:42.630
Operating cashflow for the first half totaled $3.466 billion against $28.476 billion of first half

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:49.310
capex, so the spending is being funded by the IPO and bond proceeds rather than current operations.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:57.450
After the print, SPCX fell roughly 7% in after hours trading and hovered near $117.

00:06:58.110 --> 00:07:04.330
Below the $135 IPO price and the $150 opening trade.

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:11.410
The first major share lock-up expiration begins August 6, creating a supply overhang to monitor.

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:19.310
Two customers accounted for approximately 19.5% and 18.3% of second quarter revenue,

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:23.990
illustrating concentration risk tied to the new cloud services agreements.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:32.650
Starlink average revenue per user declined from $85 to $66 year over year as part of the scale strategy.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:39.730
The CFO's statement that the company is on a trajectory to reach $100 billion of annualized

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:45.790
recurring revenue by year end should be treated as a management projection whose definition and timing

00:07:45.790 --> 00:07:52.670
carry the usual caveats around backlog conversion and contract wrap. Commentary following the release

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:58.630
highlighted the revenue beat and subscriber growth as impressive while focusing on the scale of AI

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:06.050
related capex as the primary driver of the after hours decline. Outlets such as CNBC noted that

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:12.870
soaring AI costs weighed on the stock despite the top line outperformance, with investors emphasizing

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:19.570
near-term cash burn. X commentary from accounts including at Homo Trades described the revenue

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:26.770
trajectory as strong yet flagged the capex debate as the dominant concern. Other posts observed that

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:31.730
Nvidia remains positioned as a direct beneficiary of the infrastructure build out.

00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:38.470
The sharpest takes centered on whether the AI revenue quality and long-term utilization justify

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:43.090
the quarterly spend rate rather than on the launch or starlink results themselves.

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:50.070
From an engineering standpoint, the $15.828 billion in quarterly AI infrastructure

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:57.090
capex appears on the balance sheet as servers and networking equipment rising from $22.7 billion

00:08:57.650 --> 00:09:05.470
to $34.8 billion in six months and construction and progress nearly tripling to $12.6 billion

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:13.530
while nameplate compute capacity scaled from 0.4 gigawatts to 1.4 gigawatts in a single year.

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:19.350
A gigawatt class data center draws continuous power on the order of a large city,

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:23.710
whereas a single starship launch consumes energy measured in minutes.

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:32.230
The depreciation line in the AI segment reached $1.885 billion per quarter because chips lose value

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:37.750
on a schedule measured in years rather than the flight-proven reusability of rocket engines.

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:44.430
Connectivity operating leverage stems from the fact that 28 of the 38 second quarter launches

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:50.350
were internal Falcon flights carrying starlink satellites at marginal cost once the vehicle and

00:09:50.350 --> 00:09:56.250
pad are already operating. The same first principles approach that drove Raptor engine cost

00:09:56.250 --> 00:10:01.930
reduction is now being applied to data center power density and networking with the vertical

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:07.290
integration of launch, satellite production and compute hardware intended to compress the cost

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:15.190
curve across both orbital and terrestrial infrastructure. SPCX traded near $117 in the

00:10:15.190 --> 00:10:21.970
Sessions after the release below the IPO reference levels. Forward items include the flight 14

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:28.350
tower catch attempt window at the end of August. The cursor acquisition closed expected in the third

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:34.910
quarter. The next two quarters of elevated CAPEX, the ongoing lockup expiration calendar and the

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:40.570
trajectories for starlink subscriber additions and compute capacity expansion toward the stated

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:46.930
two gigawatt target by year end. The quarter showed a launch cadence supporting internal starlink

00:10:46.930 --> 00:10:53.250
deployment, a connectivity business generating operating income and an AI segment achieving its

00:10:53.250 --> 00:11:00.330
first positive adjusted EE bit DA on the back of large contracted agreements. The same period also

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:06.650
revealed quarterly AI CAPEX running well ahead of operating cash flow and customer concentration

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:12.370
in the new revenue streams. The open question left by the filing is whether the infrastructure

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:17.690
build rate and the starship flight cadence can both scale fast enough to convert the backlog

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:24.070
and contracted compute into sustained cash generation. And that's a wrap on today's SpaceX

00:11:24.070 --> 00:11:34.310
developments. SPCX is trading at $115.80 down 0.1%. Share this with a fellow space flight fan if you

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:39.570
found it useful and subscribe so you don't miss tomorrow's episode. I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

